{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1973244044#10_2126282095","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage is about causes of intellectual disability and briefly mentions abnormal genes inherited from parents as one cause. It is related to genetic inheritance but not dedicated to explaining how hereditary diseases are passed between generations.","learned":"Some conditions can result from abnormal genes inherited from parents, but this result is too specific and incidental."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"inherited disease\" \"parents\" \"children\" genes)","reason":"Search for documents explicitly using inherited disease terminology with parents, children, and genes, likely to directly explain hereditary disease transmission."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"inherited disease\" \"parents\" \"children\" genes)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_146807254#8_351597825","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q196","rank":1,"snippet":"\": \"Health Conditions Health Conditions Genetics of Cystic Fibrosis How CF is inherited? When both parents carry a CF gene When a person with CF has a child Genetic Testing Future research Getting more information Related Topics References & Sources \" , \"segment\": \"Families may have CF carriers who do not have symptoms and have not had a child who received 2 abnormal CF genes. Most people who have children with CF did not know that CF <cover>genes ran in their families. Cystic fibrosis was only recognized as an inherited disease in the 1930s. Many families may not have known about children who had CF and died in past generations. When both parents </cover>carry a CF gene When a man and a woman who are both CF carriers conceive a child, 1 of 3 things happens: There is a 1-in-4 chance (25% of the time) the child will receive a CF gene from each parent (two abnormal genes total) and will be born with CF disease. There is a 2-in-4 chance (50% of the time) the child will receive 1 CF gene from 1 parent and a non-CF normal gene "}}
