{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_146807254#8_351597825","relevance":2,"reason":"The document is dedicated to cystic fibrosis inheritance and directly explains parent-to-child transmission of abnormal CF genes with 25%/50% probabilities, but it is specific to one inherited disease rather than generally explaining how hereditary diseases are passed between generations.","learned":"Cystic fibrosis is inherited when a child receives abnormal CF genes from parents; if both parents are carriers, each pregnancy can produce an affected child, a carrier child, or an unaffected non-carrier child."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q196","reason":"The query is retrieving disease-inheritance explanations with parents, children, and genes; subsequent results may be broader or similarly strong direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q196"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_38_179952724#5_380121545","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q196","rank":2,"snippet":"of Beta Thalassemia Inheritance of Alpha Thalassemia \" , \"segment\": \"The inheritance of alpha thalassemia is complicated because of the mutation of two different genes HBA1 and HBA2. There are two copies of the HBA1 gene and two copies of the HBA2 gene in each cell. For each gene, one copy is inherited from the mother and the other copy is inherited from the father. If each of the <cover>parents lacks at least one gene copy, their children are at a higher risk for having alpha thalassemia. The risk and the severity of each child's condition depend on the number of gene copies lost (deleted) and which combination of the HBA1 and HBA2 genes are affected. Why is Thalassemia an Inherited Disease? </cover>Thalassemia is an inherited condition. The genes inherited from the parents prior to birth determine whether a person will have thalassemia or not. The clinical severity of thalassemia varies tremendously on the basis of the exact nature of the genes inherited by a person. At the time of conception, a person receives one set of genes from the mother and another set of genes from the father.\" , \"start_char\""}}
