{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"inherited genetic conditions\" \"passed down\" families genes)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1162195692#1_2349024189","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q205","rank":1,"snippet":"end_char\": 1456.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Are the Risk Factors for Leukemia? What Are the Risk Factors for Leukemia? Genes Viruses Family History Environmental Causes Blood Disorders Is Leukemia Preventable? NEWSLETTERS Sign up for our Cancer Care Newsletter! Enter your email Editorial Sources and Fact-Checking \" , \"segment\": \"1)<cover>Genes Certain inherited genetic conditions and immune disorders can put a person at greater risk for developing leukemia. These may include: Down syndrome People with Down syndrome have an extra copy of chromosome 21. Kids with Down are 20 times more likely to develop acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL) than the general population. They’re also more at risk for having acute myeloid leukemia (AML). ( 2) Fanconi anemia This disease is passed down through families </cover>and causes a decreased production of blood cells. It can also raise a person’s risk of developing leukemia. Ataxia-telangiectasia Ataxia-telangiectasia is an inherited condition that affects a person’s immune system. It increases your risk of infection and leukemia. Li-Fraumeni syndrome This rare condition happens because of a change in the TP53 tumor suppressor gene.\" , \"start_"}}
