{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#0_2833057255"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#0_2833057255","document":"How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited ... How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited Understanding Patterns of Inheritance Patterns of Inheritance Autosomal Dominant Autosomal Recessive X-Linked Dominant X-Linked Recessive Mitochondrial Other Inheritance Patterns Y-Linked Disorders Codominance  ... How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited Rare Diseases Genetic Disorders How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited Understanding Patterns of Inheritance By Cherie Berkley, MS twitter Cherie Berkley is an award-winning journalist and multimedia storyteller covering health features for Verywell. Learn about our editorial process Cherie Berkley, MS Medically reviewed by Medically reviewed by Elizabeth Molina Ortiz, MD, MPH on September 25, 2020 linkedin Elizabeth Molina Ortiz, MD, MPH, is a board-certified specialist in family medicine and is the former medical director of a community health center. Learn about our Medical Review Board Elizabeth Molina Ortiz, MD, MPH on September 25, 2020 Table of Contents View All Table of Contents Patterns of Inheritance Genetic disorders are precisely what they sound like: Diseases caused by a mutation of a gene. When such diseases are inherited (rather than the result of a random mutation), it means they are passed along to a child from one or both parents according to a specific patterns of inheritance. These patterns are determined by the gene involved, whether only one or both parents have the gene, which chromosome it is on, and other factors. 1 ﻿ The presence of a mutation doesn't always translate to the disease it's associated with. For example, Huntington's disease, breast cancer, and autoimmune diseases are associated with specific genes, but a person who inherits them won't necessarily develop these conditions. On the other hand, some genetic mutations, such as those linked to hemophilia, will always manifest the disorder. Furthermore, the environment can have an effect on the degree to which a gene mutation is expressed, which explains why in some cases family members with the same genetic mutation may experience an inherited disorder somewhat differently.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"32/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#0_2833057255","relevance":3,"reason":"The full Verywell document is explicitly titled 'How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited' and directly explains that inherited genetic disorders are diseases caused by gene mutations passed to a child from one or both parents according to specific inheritance patterns determined by the gene, chromosome, and parental carrier status.","learned":"Inherited genetic disorders are caused by gene mutations passed from one or both parents to a child; inheritance patterns depend on the gene involved, whether one or both parents carry it, which chromosome it is on, and other factors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q209","reason":"This query retrieved a dedicated grade-3 document on genetic-disorder inheritance; continue for additional segments from the same or similar pages that may directly explain autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, X-linked, or other inheritance mechanisms."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q209"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#1_2833059644","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q209","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#1_2833059644\" , \"end_char\": 2379.000000 , \"headings\": \"How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited<cover>How Genetic Disorders Are Inherited Understanding Patterns of Inheritance Patterns of Inheritance Autosomal Dominant Autosomal Recessive</cover>X-Linked Dominant X-Linked Recessive Mitochondrial Other Inheritance Patterns Y-Linked Disorders Codominance \" , \"segment\": \"These patterns are determined by the gene involved, whether only one or both parents have the gene, which chromosome it is on, and other factors. 1 ﻿ The presence of a mutation doesn't always translate to the disease it's associated with. For example, Huntington's disease, breast cancer, and autoimmune diseases are associated with specific genes, but a person who inherits them won't necessarily develop these conditions. On the other hand, some genetic mutations, such as those linked to hemophilia, will always manifest the disorder. Furthermore, the environment can have an effect on the degree to which a gene mutation is expressed, which explains why in some cases family members with the same genetic mutation may experience an inherited disorder somewhat differently. 2 ﻿ cdascher / Getty "}}
