{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The previous query returned no results to judge.","learned":"The exact phrase 'gene mutations passed from parents to children' with genetic disorders was too restrictive."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"mutations\" \"passed from parent to child\" \"genetic\")","reason":"Use a less restrictive phrase to find pages explaining that genetic mutations can be passed from parent to child, directly supporting hereditary disease transmission."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"mutations\" \"passed from parent to child\" \"genetic\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_579248823#4_1133782683","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q215","rank":1,"snippet":"when their symptoms began how early they began treatment which treatments they receive their overall health More research is needed to learn how different treatment approaches may affect survival rates and life expectancy in people with this condition. Types of ATTR amyloidosis The type of ATTR amyloidosis that a person has will affect their long-term outlook. If you’re living with ATTR amyloidosis, but you’re not sure which type, ask your doctor. The two main types are familial and wild-type. Other types of amyloidosis may also develop when proteins other than TTR clump into amyloid fibrils. Familial ATTR amyloidosis Familial ATTR amyloidosis is also known as hereditary ATTR amyloidosis. It’s caused by genetic mutations that may be <cover>passed from parent to child. These genetic mutations </cover>cause TTR to be less stable than normal. This raises the chances that TTR will form amyloid fibrils. Many different genetic mutations may cause familial ATTR amyloidosis.\" , \"start_char\": 2091.000000 , \"title\": \"ATTR Amyloidosis Life Expectancy: Survival Rates and More\" , \"url\": \"https://www.healthline.com/health/amyloidosis/life-expectancy-attr-amyloidosis\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"38/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_579248823#4_1133782683","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains a specific hereditary disease, familial ATTR amyloidosis, as caused by genetic mutations passed from parent to child. It supports the mechanism but is disease-specific rather than dedicated to hereditary diseases in general.","learned":"Hereditary diseases can be caused by genetic mutations that are passed from parent to child; those mutations can alter protein function and cause disease."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"genetic disorders\" \"caused by\" mutation inherited parents)","reason":"Search for broad medical overviews of genetic disorders that explain they are caused by mutations and may be inherited from parents, likely to yield additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"genetic disorders\" \"caused by\" mutation inherited parents)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1038541677#1_2286740287","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q216","rank":1,"snippet":"are not inherited from a parent, but occur either randomly or due to some environmental exposure (such as cigarette smoke). These include many cancers, as well as some forms of neurofibromatosis. Overview A genetic disorder is a disease caused in whole or in part by a change in the DNA sequence away from the normal sequence. <cover>Genetic disorders can be caused by a mutation in one gene (monogenic disorder), by mutations in multiple genes (multifactorial inheritance disorder), by a combination of gene mutations and environmental factors, or by damage to chromosomes (changes in the number or structure of entire chromosomes, the structures that carry genes). As we unlock the secrets of the human genome (the complete set of human genes), we are learning that nearly all diseases have a genetic component. Some diseases are caused by mutations that are inherited from the parents </cover>and are present in an individual at birth, like sickle cell disease. Other diseases are caused by acquired mutations in a gene or group of genes that occur during a person's life. Such mutations are not inherited from a parent, but occur either randomly or due to some environmental exposure (such as cigarette smoke).\" ,"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"39/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_20_1038541677#1_2286740287","relevance":3,"reason":"The broad genetic-disorder overview directly explains that genetic disorders are caused by DNA sequence changes, including mutations in one or many genes or chromosomal damage, and states that some diseases are caused by mutations inherited from parents and present at birth. This directly supports how hereditary diseases pass between generations.","learned":"Genetic disorders arise from DNA, gene, or chromosome changes; hereditary forms are caused by mutations inherited from parents and present from birth, while acquired mutations are not inherited."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q216","reason":"This query produced a strong general genetic-disorder overview; continue to see if more results directly explain inherited mutations and parent-to-child disease transmission."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q216"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1077824783#0_1188711907","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q216","rank":2,"snippet":"caused by one or more abnormalities in the genome. It can be caused by a mutation in a single gene (monogenic) or multiple genes (polygenic) or by a chromosomal abnormality. Although polygenic disorders are the most common, the term is mostly used when discussing disorders with a single genetic cause, either in a gene or chromosome. The mutation responsible can occur spontaneously before embryonic development (a de novo mutation), or it can be inherited from two parents who are carriers of a faulty gene ( autosomal recessive inheritance) or from a parent with the disorder ( autosomal dominant inheritance). When the genetic disorder is inherited from one or both <cover>parents, it is also classified as a hereditary disease. Some disorders are caused by a mutation on the X chromosome and have X-linked inheritance. Very few disorders are inherited on the Y chromosome or mitochondrial DNA. There are well over 6,000 known genetic disorders, </cover>and new genetic disorders are constantly being described in medical literature.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Genetic disorder - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_disease\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"40/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1077824783#0_1188711907","relevance":3,"reason":"The genetic-disorder overview directly explains that genetic disorders can be caused by single-gene, multiple-gene, or chromosomal abnormalities; mutations may be inherited from carrier parents or an affected parent; inherited genetic disorders are hereditary diseases; and X-linked, Y-linked, and mitochondrial inheritance also occur.","learned":"Hereditary diseases are genetic disorders inherited from one or both parents; mechanisms include autosomal recessive inheritance from carrier parents, autosomal dominant inheritance from an affected parent, and X-linked, Y-linked, or mitochondrial inheritance."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q216","reason":"The current query is producing broad, direct explanations of genetic disorders caused by mutations inherited from parents, so continue for additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q216"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1613101379#8_3389434174","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q216","rank":3,"snippet":"uniparental disomy, a phenomenon whereby a child is conceived who carries the normal complement of chromosomes but who has inherited both copies of a given chromosome from the same parent, rather than one from each parent, as is the normal fashion. If any key genes on that chromosome are imprinted in the parent of origin, the child may end up with no expressed copies, and a genetic disorder may result. Similarly, other genes may be overexpressed in cases of uniparental disomy, perhaps also leading to clinical complications. Finally, uniparental disomy can account for very rare instances whereby two <cover>parents, only one of whom is a carrier of an autosomal recessive mutation, can nonetheless have an affected child, in the circumstance that the child inherits two mutant copies from the carrier parent. Diseases caused by multifactorial inheritance Genetic disorders that are multifactorial in origin represent probably the single largest class of inherited </cover>disorders affecting the human population.\" , \"start_char\": 6954.000000 , \"title\": \"Human genetic disease - Sex-linked inheritance | Britannica\" , \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/science/human-genetic-disease/Sex-linked-inheritance\" }"}}
