{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_428726263#3_1027578661","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage broadly explains genetic disorders and autosomal recessive inheritance: genetic disorders may be caused by gene mutations, each person inherits one copy of each gene from each parent, carriers have one normal and one mutated copy, and if both parents pass the mutation to a baby the baby will have the disorder. This directly supports how hereditary diseases are passed between generations.","learned":"In autosomal recessive hereditary disorders, each person has two gene copies inherited from parents; carrier parents may each pass a mutation to a child, and inheriting two mutated copies causes the disorder."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q216","reason":"The current query is producing broad, direct explanations of genetic disorders caused by inherited mutations and inheritance patterns, so continue for additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q216"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_684548888#4_1430061360","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q216","rank":7,"snippet":"known as inherited or genetic diseases. Sometimes these genetic malformations are passed down from one set of genes of the parent to the offspring and at other times, there is a spontaneous change in the DNA that affects the formation of these genes. Due to the spontaneous change in the gene of the offspring, it is entirely possible to have a child with <cover>genetic disorders whereas the parents are healthy with no genetic malformations. This condition is known as a new mutation. These new changes in the gene can cause the production of a protein which doesn’t function properly or the non-production of a protein which can irrevocably lead to inherited diseases. For each inherited disease, the presentation of the genetic makeup is different. For some diseases, all the mistakes that are caused by </cover>a particular gene result in a single genetic disorder. However, for other cases, the different changes on each of these genes can cause different health, developmental or mental problems. It can even give rise to different genetic disorders.\" , \"start_char\": 2281.000000 , \"title\": \"Inherited Diseases: Types, Causes and Symptoms\" , \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1419815345#0_2833057255","rank":6,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_684548888#4_1430061360","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is titled 'Inherited Diseases: Types, Causes and Symptoms' and is dedicated to inherited/genetic diseases. It directly explains that genetic malformations can be passed from parental genes to offspring, while some disorders arise from new DNA mutations in otherwise healthy parents.","learned":"Inherited diseases can be passed from parent genes to offspring, but some genetic disorders arise from new/spontaneous mutations that affect gene function or protein production."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q216","reason":"This query continues to produce broad, direct explanations of genetic disorders and inherited mutations, so continue to collect additional grade-3 evidence within the remaining judgment budget."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q216"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_247476520#12_478311990","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q216","rank":8,"snippet":"recessive genetic disorder. Dominant genetic disorders occur when only a single copy of an abnormal gene is necessary to cause a particular disease. The abnormal gene can be inherited from either parent or can be the result of a new mutation in the affected individual. The risk of passing the abnormal gene from an affected parent to an offspring is 50% for each pregnancy. The risk is the same for males and females. In some individuals, the disorder is due to a spontaneous ( de novo) genetic <cover>mutation that occurs in the egg or sperm cell. In such situations, the disorder is not inherited from the parents. X-linked genetic disorders are conditions caused by </cover>an abnormal gene on the X chromosome and manifest mostly in males. Females that have an altered gene present on one of their X chromosomes are carriers for that disorder. Carrier females usually do not display symptoms because females have two X chromosomes and only one carries the altered gene.\" , \"start_char\": 6361.000000 , \"title\": \"Amelogenesis Imperfecta - NORD (National Organization for Rare Disorders)\" , \"url\": \"https://rarediseases.org/rare-"}}
