{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"chromosomal disorders\" inherited parents children)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1176684116#1_2645092034","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q219","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1176684116#1_2645092034\" , \"end_char\": 1768.000000 , \"headings\": \" How Is DNA <cover>Inherited From Each Parent? Take This Quiz Q.1. Is it possible to inherit chromosomal disorders? Q.2. What is DNA testing? Related Reading: Interesting Reads: Was this answer helpful? \" , \"segment\": \"The new offspring inherits exactly half the DNA from each of their parents, while each parent passes half their DNA to each of their children. </cover>During sexual fertilization, DNA from both the parents combine. This assures that the families pass on and maintain the genetic identity, but there is a genetic difference either with their parents or their paternal and maternal grandparents so much so that they are also different genetically from their own sisters and brothers unless it is a case of identical twins. Human DNA has a history of our ancestors. The mix of the DNA is unique to an individual as we receive 50% of the DNA from each of the parent, who received 50% of their DNA from their parents and so on which keeps "}}
