{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1176684116#1_2645092034","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage explains the general mechanism of DNA inheritance, stating that offspring inherit half their DNA from each parent and that parental DNA combines during fertilization. This supports how hereditary information passes between generations, but it is not dedicated to hereditary diseases and does not directly explain disease-causing gene or chromosome variants.","learned":"Offspring inherit about half their DNA from each parent through sexual fertilization, providing the biological basis for passing genetic traits and potential disease-causing variants across generations."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"mitochondrial inheritance\" \"genetic disorders\" mother children)","reason":"Search for authoritative evidence on mitochondrial inheritance, another major pathway by which hereditary genetic disorders can pass between generations, especially from mothers to children."}}
